    "body": "View of the open pit at Cuiú Cuiú in Brazil. Credit: Cabral Gold.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 19, 2026\nCabral Gold (TSXV: CBR, US-OTC: CBGZF) has lifted measured and indicated gold resources by 49% to just under 1 million oz. at Cuiú Cuiú in northern Brazil, where the company poured first gold in September. The stock soared.\nThe estimate, effective Nov. 11, puts measured and indicated resources at 42.5 million tonnes grading 0.73 gram gold per tonne for 998,800 oz., according to a statement Thursday. Inferred resources rose 376% to 82.1 million tonnes grading 0.95 gram gold per tonne for 2.51 million oz. The estimate covers six deposits about 195 km south-southwest of Itaituba in Pará state. “All six of the deposits identified to date are open at depth, and several are open along strike,” CEO Alan Carter said in the statement.\nCabral shares jumped about 13% to $1.27 Thursday morning on the TSX Venture Exchange, valuing the company at about $441 million (US$308 million). The stock has traded between 55¢ and $1.68 in the past year.\nSecond phase\nMost of the gold in the updated estimate lies in hard rock beneath the shallow oxide ore that feeds Cabral’s new mine.\nThe heap-leach operation is designed to treat 3,000 tonnes of oxide ore a day. Measured and indicated gold in that material grew 21% to 265,600 oz., while inferred ounces rose 190% to 210,900.\nA planned second phase would target hard rock containing 733,200 indicated oz. and 2.3 million inferred oz., up 63% and 406%, respectively, from the 2022 estimate. The new estimate used a long-term gold price of US$3,500 ($5,005) per oz. to set the limits of the modelled pits. Cabral plans infill drilling in 2027 to move some of the inferred material into higher-confidence resource categories.\nAlluvial gold\nLocated in the Tapajós gold district, Cuiú Cuiú covers 306 sq. km. Cabral’s Brazilian subsidiary estimates that small-scale miners recovered up to 2 million oz. of alluvial gold at Cuiú Cuiú during the region’s gold rush. The company has worked in the area since 2017. Cabral started with an open-pit mine that leaches gold from weathered rock near the surface. Its July 2025 prefeasibility study put pre-production capital at US$37.7 million and projected about 113,000 oz. of gold over 6.2 years from the oxide operation. Vancouver-based Cabral received the mine’s operating licence in August and reported its first sale of more than 2,400 oz. of gold to a Brazilian refinery in September.\nIt said at the time that it expected to reach commercial production by year-end. Thursday’s update said the mine was still ramping up.\nCabral also holds the Bom Jardim exploration property about 25 km northwest of Cuiú Cuiú. It controls two exploration licences covering more than 13,000 hectares in the former alluvial mining district, where it has conducted geophysical surveys, sampling and limited drilling.\nPrint\nTAG: Brazil gold gold exploration gold mines gold mining gold production gold projects\nCOMMODITY: Gold\nREGION: Canada South America\nCOMPANY: Cabral Gold\nRepublish this article",
